    "question": "Classify and compare the different types of domestic electrical wiring systems: (i) Cleat wiring, (ii) Wooden Casing and Capping, (iii) CTS / Batten wiring, and (iv) Conduit wiring. State which wiring system is recommended for modern residential installations and why.",
    "diagramTitle": "Comparison of Domestic Electrical Installation Wiring Systems",
    "diagramSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 160\" class=\"w-full h-auto max-w-lg mx-auto\">\n      <rect x=\"20\" y=\"20\" width=\"105\" height=\"120\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#b45309\" />\n      <text x=\"25\" y=\"45\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#92400e\">Cleat Wiring</text>\n      <text x=\"25\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"8\" fill=\"#475569\">Temporary only</text>\n      <text x=\"25\" y=\"90\" font-size=\"8\" fill=\"#475569\">Porcelain cleats</text>\n      <text x=\"25\" y=\"110\" font-size=\"8\" fill=\"#dc2626\">Zero fire safety</text>\n      <rect x=\"135\" y=\"20\" width=\"105\" height=\"120\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#b45309\" />\n      <text x=\"140\" y=\"45\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#92400e\">Casing-Capping</text>\n      <text x=\"140\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"8\" fill=\"#475569\">Teakwood / PVC</text>\n      <text x=\"140\" y=\"90\" font-size=\"8\" fill=\"#475569\">Grooved channels</text>\n      <text x=\"140\" y=\"110\" font-size=\"8\" fill=\"#dc2626\">Obsolete</text>\n      <rect x=\"250\" y=\"20\" width=\"105\" height=\"120\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#b45309\" />\n      <text x=\"255\" y=\"45\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#92400e\">Batten Wiring</text>\n      <text x=\"255\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"8\" fill=\"#475569\">CTS/TRS cables</text>\n      <text x=\"255\" y=\"90\" font-size=\"8\" fill=\"#475569\">Tinned brass clips</text>\n      <text x=\"255\" y=\"110\" font-size=\"8\" fill=\"#475569\">Fair lifespan</text>\n      <rect x=\"365\" y=\"20\" width=\"115\" height=\"120\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#f0fdf4\" stroke=\"#059669\" stroke-width=\"2\" />\n      <text x=\"375\" y=\"45\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#166534\">Conduit Wiring</text>\n      <text x=\"375\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"8\" fill=\"#059669\">Concealed PVC/GI</text>\n      <text x=\"375\" y=\"90\" font-size=\"8\" fill=\"#059669\">100% Fireproof</text>\n      <text x=\"375\" y=\"110\" font-size=\"8\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#059669\">MODERN CHOICE</text>\n    </svg>",
    "modelAnswer": "CLASSIFICATION OF INTERNAL DOMESTIC WIRING SYSTEMS:\n\n1. CLEAT WIRING:\n• Construction: VIR or PVC insulated wires run across walls and ceilings supported by two-part porcelain cleats spaced at 30 to 60 cm intervals.\n• Advantages: Very cheap, quick installation, easily inspected, dismantled, and recovered.\n• Disadvantages: Completely exposed to mechanical damage, dust, moisture, and fire. Unattractive appearance.\n• Application: Strictly temporary wiring (construction sites, army camps, outdoor events).\n\n2. WOODEN CASING AND CAPPING WIRING:\n• Construction: Wires are laid inside longitudinal grooves of seasoned teakwood (or PVC) casing and covered by a capping strip screwed in place.\n• Disadvantages: High fire hazard (wood is combustible), prone to moisture ingress, termite attack, and expensive skilled carpentry. Now virtually obsolete.\n\n3. BATTE / CTS / TRS WIRING:\n• Construction: Tough Rubber Sheathed (TRS) or Cab Tyre Sheathed (CTS) cables run over varnished wooden battens nailed to walls and held by tinned brass link clips spaced at 10 cm.\n• Disadvantages: Moderate fire risk, exposed cables deteriorate under sunlight.\n\n4. CONDUIT WIRING (SURFACE & CONCEALED):\n• Construction: PVC insulated cables are pulled through rigid PVC or Galvanized Iron (GI) conduit pipes using GI fish wire.\n  - Surface Conduit: Pipes clamped on exterior wall surfaces.\n  - Concealed Conduit: Pipes chased inside wall brickwork and plaster during building construction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRECOMMENDED SYSTEM: CONCEALED CONDUIT WIRING\nConcealed PVC Conduit Wiring is universally recommended for all modern residential and commercial buildings for the following reasons:\n1. Complete Fire Safety: Heavy-duty PVC conduits are flame-retardant and self-extinguishing, preventing electrical fire spread.\n2. Shock Protection & Mechanical Durability: High-impact conduits protect wires against rodent gnawing, nails, dampness, and physical damage.\n3. Aesthetic Excellence: Conduits are completely invisible behind plaster, preserving interior architectural beauty.\n4. Rewirability: Defective cables can be pulled out and new wires pulled through without chipping or damaging finished walls.",
